# Upstream commits a mix of line endings: the vcpkg overlay triplets are LF, the
# .cpp and .props files are CRLF. `git apply` matches hunk context byte for byte,
# so a patch has to reach the build exactly as git produced it.
#
# This matters more than it looks. With core.autocrlf=true, which is the default
# on Windows and what the runner image ships, `git add` silently strips every CR
# from a patch file. The result still looks like a patch and still contains all
# the right lines, but applying it fails with "patch does not apply" against
# every CRLF file it touches. That is not a guess - it is how the first release
# build failed, on both architectures at once.
#
# Upstream protects its own tree the same way, with `* -text` in its
# .gitattributes. Do the same here, for the directory that records upstream's
# bytes.
patch/** -text

# Binary assets should never be touched either.
*.zip -text
*.exe -text